<h2 id="why-bangla-is-under-served">Why Bangla is under-served</h2><p>Bangla is one of the most widely spoken languages in the world, yet it is a small presence in the data that today’s AI systems learn from. The result is familiar to anyone who has tried a voice assistant in Bangla: it works for standard written forms and breaks down for regional speech, mixed Bangla and English, and the language of everyday public services.</p>
<p>This is not a question of talent. Bangladeshi engineers and researchers already build language tools. The gap is in the shared layer underneath: open datasets, agreed benchmarks and access to the compute needed to train and test models.</p>
<blockquote>A country cannot adopt AI responsibly in a language its AI does not understand.</blockquote>
<h2 id="three-layers-of-readiness">Three layers of readiness</h2><p><b>Data.</b> Consented, documented speech and text across dialects, released under licences that let universities and startups use it. <b>Compute.</b> Shared access to training and evaluation hardware, so that a student lab does not need its own cluster. <b>People.</b> Researchers who can evaluate models for accuracy and fairness in Bangla, not only build them.</p>
<h2 id="what-this-report-will-do">What this report will do</h2><p>The full report will map what exists today in each layer, where the gaps are, and what a coordinated national effort would need from government, universities and the technology organisations we are built to work with. It will close with a short list of first projects that the AI for Bangla Lab can start with.</p><p class="tags"><a class="tg" href="{{ route('insights.index') }}?tag=ai">#ai</a><a class="tg" href="{{ route('insights.index') }}?tag=bangla">#bangla</a><a class="tg" href="{{ route('insights.index') }}?tag=data">#data</a></p></article></div></section>
